  "text": "曆\tuser\n>(&To solve this problem, we need to understand that the sum of the squares of the divisors of a positive integer $n$ can be represented as $(1^2 + 2^2 + \\cdots + d(n))^2$, where $d(n)$ is the number of divisors of $n$. The sum of the squares of the first $k$ natural numbers is given by $\\frac{k(k + 1)(2k + 1)}{6}$, so we have:\n\n$(1^2 + 2^2 + \\cdots + d(n))^2 = 729$\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n$\\left(\\frac{d(n)(d(n) + 1)(2d(n) + 1)}{6}\\right)^2 = 729$\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\n$\\frac{d(n)(d(n) + 1)(2d(n) + 1)}{6} = 27$\n\nMultiplying both sides by 6, we get:\n\n$d(n)(d(n) + 1)(2d(n) + 1) = 162$\n\nWe need to find the value of $d(n)$ that satisfies this equation. We can do this by trial and error, testing values of $d(n)$ until we find one that works. We get:\n\n$d(n) = 6$\n\nTesting other values of $d(n)$, we find that they do not satisfy the equation. Therefore, the number of divisors of $n$ is $11$.",
